NOTE
Tutorial 1 — Getting Started: Build, Deploy, and Run QuorumProof.
Planning timestamps generated from the script's scene budgets.
Re-time against the final edit before publishing (see docs/video-tutorials.md).

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.938
Welcome to QuorumProof.

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:10.938
In this video we'll go from a fresh clone
to a running local stack in about

3
00:00:11.038 --> 00:00:13.092
eight minutes.

4
00:00:13.192 --> 00:00:20.092
QuorumProof verifies engineering credentials
using quorum slices: a university,

5
00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:27.092
a licensing body, and past employers co-
sign a soulbound token on Stellar.

6
00:00:27.192 --> 00:00:30.323
Let's get it running.

7
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.400
The project has three core Soroban
contracts.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:59.400
quorum_proof holds credentials, quorum
slices, and attestations.

9
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:06.400
sbt_registry mints the non-transferable
soulbound tokens.

10
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:13.400
zk_verifier checks zero-knowledge proofs
so holders can prove a claim without

11
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.010
revealing the whole credential.

12
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:26.010
The architecture doc explains how they fit
together.

13
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:52.400
Clone the repository and add the
WebAssembly target.

14
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:59.400
The build script compiles every contract
to WASM.

15
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:06.400
Then run cargo test.

16
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:13.400
The first build takes a few minutes; after
that it's incremental.

17
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.400
All tests should pass before you change
anything.

18
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:37.400
Next, create a testnet identity called
deployer.

19
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:44.400
The Stellar CLI generates a key and funds
it from Friendbot.

20
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:51.400
Never reuse this key on mainnet, and never
paste a secret key into a terminal

21
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.365
you're recording.

22
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.721
Now deploy.

23
00:04:35.821 --> 00:04:42.721
The script uploads each contract,
initializes it, and wires them together.

24
00:04:42.821 --> 00:04:49.721
At the end it prints the contract IDs.

25
00:04:49.821 --> 00:04:56.721
Copy them; the API server needs them in
its environment file.

26
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:07.400
Copy the example environment file at the
repository root and paste in the

27
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.517
contract IDs.

28
00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:18.517
Then move into the api-server folder,
install dependencies, and start the dev

29
00:06:18.617 --> 00:06:20.735
server.

30
00:06:20.835 --> 00:06:27.735
Finally, call the health endpoint.

31
00:06:27.835 --> 00:06:34.735
A healthy status means the server can
reach the database and the Soroban RPC.

32
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.532
That's it.

33
00:07:32.632 --> 00:07:39.532
You built the contracts, deployed them to
testnet, and started the API.

34
00:07:39.632 --> 00:07:46.532
In the next video we'll create your first
quorum slice.
